<h2>The statutory text points backward as well as forward</h2>
<p>Three definitions carry the weight. An "aggrieved person" under Section 2(a) is a woman who is, <em>or has been</em>, in a domestic relationship with the respondent and alleges domestic violence. A "domestic relationship" under Section 2(f) exists between persons who live <em>or have, at any point of time, lived</em> together in a shared household. And "shared household" under Section 2(s) includes a household the aggrieved person has lived in with the respondent in a domestic relationship. The past tense is not an accident of drafting — it is the Act\'s answer to the argument that separation or divorce closes the door.</p>

<h2>What the Supreme Court settled in Prabha Tyagi</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>No subsisting cohabitation needed</h4><p>It is not mandatory for the aggrieved person to have been living with the respondents when the application is filed; the domestic relationship concept embraces past living arrangements.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Right to reside is expansive</h4><p>Under Section 17(1), every woman in a domestic relationship has the right to reside in the shared household regardless of title, and the right is not confined to actual residence — constructive residence counts.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>No DIR is required to start</h4><p>Where the woman files directly or through counsel, the Magistrate need not await a Domestic Incident Report; the proviso to Section 12(1) applies only when a DIR has been received from a Protection Officer or service provider.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Mapping the reliefs after the marriage ends</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Relief</th><th>Provision</th><th>Position after divorce/separation</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Protection order</td><td>Section 18</td><td>Available where acts of domestic violence are shown and protection is needed against continuing harassment.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Residence order</td><td>Sections 17, 19</td><td>Fact-sensitive: the claim relates to the shared household of the past relationship, and divorce-settlement terms, alternative accommodation and current occupation all weigh in the balance.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monetary relief</td><td>Section 20</td><td>Available for losses and expenses attributable to the violence; maintenance components interact with orders under other laws and with any settlement.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Compensation</td><td>Section 22</td><td>Available for injuries, including mental torture and emotional distress, caused by acts committed during the relationship.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reliefs for children</td><td>Sections 20, 21</td><td>A child\'s claims are independent and are not defeated by the parents\' divorce or by waivers the mother gave for herself.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The limits: what post-divorce applications cannot do</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No retrospective relationship.</strong> The Act presupposes a shared household at some point; it gives no remedy where none ever existed.</li>
    <li><strong>No revival of waived claims.</strong> Monetary claims consciously relinquished in a comprehensive settlement cannot be re-routed through a fresh DV application.</li>
    <li><strong>No substitute for matrimonial remedies.</strong> Questions of the divorce itself, permanent alimony under matrimonial statutes, and custody in the full sense belong to their own forums; the DV Act supplements, it does not replace.</li>
    <li><strong>Delay invites scrutiny.</strong> There is no rigid limitation for seeking civil reliefs, but a long-unexplained gap between the relationship\'s end and the application affects credibility and interim relief.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Practice notes for Delhi proceedings</h2>
<p>Applications under Section 12 are filed before the Magistrate within whose jurisdiction the aggrieved person resides, works, or where the respondent resides or the cause of action arose — a venue flexibility that survives the divorce. Pleadings should anchor each relief to specific acts committed during the relationship, with dates, and should squarely disclose any settlement or matrimonial litigation, since suppression is the fastest route to dismissal. Respondents, for their part, should resist the reflex of a maintainability objection built only on the decree of divorce: after Prabha Tyagi, the contest is won or lost on the evidence of violence and on the effect of settlements, not on the subsistence of the marriage.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. The availability of particular reliefs depends on the facts, the settlement history and the orders already passed between the parties.</p></div>
